Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-13-2000||10:02 PM||||Has anyone explored the possibility of building up a carbed 2.8?  There's some interesting stuff in the GM performance parts catalogue that might work nicely.  Like the two barrel intake manifold #14044991.  How bout mounting a Holley on it? There's a couple of nice cams too.  Some reliability and gas mileage would be lost, but what kind of gains do you think could be expected?||||reg||14
Z||000001||mshill||01-14-2000||02:56 PM||||My experience of having gone from a carb to TBI on my 4.3L V6 in my boat.  I can't imagine that a carb could provide better performance than the MPFI that the Fiero has.<P>From what I understand, the Fiero intake is very well designed and you can put a performance cam (Crane Compucam, Wolverine) that will work with the stock computer.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||01-14-2000||05:05 PM||||Someone here,awhile back,mentioned using a 290DEG performance cam,with good results on an injected motor.All that was changed was th fuel pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-14-2000||07:30 PM||||Welcome back LowCG! Even if the ECM could handle that big of a cam, you'd have very little low end torque. The peak HP would be at a very high RPM, probably higher than the stock valvetrain can handle. High-performance light-weight high-reving valvetrains are expensive. Forget using an automatic and even with a manual, it will be a pain to drive in stop and go traffic. It would make a fun, open road playtoy though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The main advantage to using a carb engine is to bypass the computer. This is especially important if you are building an extreme high performance and/or racing engine. Fuel injection has many great advantages, but more headaches with sensors etc. If this car is a daily driver, I'd stay with the fuel injection.||||reg||1
